---
/**
 * /methodology — deeper detail than editorial-policy on how visits and
 * rankings actually happen.
 *
 * Two voice-bearing sections (visit workflow + ranking workflow) marked
 * [OWEN INPUT NEEDED]. The "trust signals" section is safe to draft because
 * it explains the schema fields baked into every piece (visit_date, photo
 * EXIF, honorable mentions, the honest critique).
 */

import TrustPageLayout from '../layouts/TrustPageLayout.astro';
import {
  buildArticleSchema,
  buildBreadcrumbSchema,
} from '../lib/schema';

const publishedDate = new Date('2026-05-14');
const lastUpdated = new Date('2026-05-14');

const title = 'Methodology — bhamranked';
const description =
  'How a visit happens, how the ranking gets assembled, and what each trust signal on a bhamranked piece means.';

const schemaBlocks = [
  buildArticleSchema({
    title,
    description,
    slug: '/methodology',
    published: publishedDate,
    modified: lastUpdated,
    type: 'Article',
  }),
  buildBreadcrumbSchema([
    { name: 'Home', url: '/' },
    { name: 'Methodology', url: '/methodology' },
  ]),
];
---

<TrustPageLayout
  title={title}
  description={description}
  kicker="Methodology"
  dek="The longer version of how bhamranked pieces get made. What a visit looks like, how the ranking is assembled, and what the trust signals on every piece actually mean."
  lastUpdated={lastUpdated}
  schemaBlocks={schemaBlocks}
>
  <section class="section">
    <h2>How a visit happens</h2>
    <p class="owen-input">
      <span class="owen-input-label">[OWEN INPUT NEEDED: 200-300 words]</span>
      Walk through what you actually do when you visit a spot. Solo or with
      friends? How do you take notes (phone, notebook, after the fact)? Do you
      photograph the food on site, with what device, do you keep EXIF on so
      timestamps are verifiable? What do you order, and how do you decide
      between an obvious dish and a deeper-cut menu item? How long do you sit?
      Do you talk to staff? Do you pay attention to the room, the music, the
      bathroom?
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <h2>How the ranking gets assembled</h2>
    <p class="owen-input">
      <span class="owen-input-label">[OWEN INPUT NEEDED: 150-250 words]</span>
      The workflow from raw notes to a published piece. How do visits across
      several months condense into one ordered list? How do you decide which
      spots make the cut versus the honorable-mentions floor? Mention that AI
      assistance is used for research and structure only, not for first-person
      content or invented visit details. Anything you want a reader to know
      about the gap between "I went there and ate" and "this piece is live."
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <h2>What the trust signals mean</h2>
    <p>
      Every piece on bhamranked carries a fixed set of signals so a reader can
      verify what they're looking at without taking anything on faith.
    </p>

    <h3>The no-paid-placement pill</h3>
    <p>
      At the top of every piece, above the headline. The same statement
      appears verbatim on every piece because it's the same rule on every
      piece. If you ever see a piece without it, that piece is broken.
    </p>

    <h3>The visit date range</h3>
    <p>
      Listicle pages show the date range of the visits that produced the
      ranking. Feature pages show one or more visit dates for the spot in
      question. These dates are the dates of actual visits, not the date the
      piece was published or last edited.
    </p>

    <h3>The last-verified date</h3>
    <p>
      Separate from the visit dates. Last-verified is when the piece was
      checked against current reality. If a business has changed hours, moved,
      or closed, the piece gets updated and the last-verified date advances.
      When last-verified is more than 6 months old, a revisit happens before
      that date updates.
    </p>

    <h3>The honest critique</h3>
    <p>
      Every spot in every piece has a labeled "honest critique" section. No
      place is perfect, and pretending otherwise would defeat the point of
      this site. The critique is the thing the editor would warn a friend
      about before recommending the spot.
    </p>

    <h3>The honorable mentions</h3>
    <p>
      Spots that were visited but didn't make the ranked list. Including them
      shows the work: this is the set of places that were considered, not just
      the set that won. If a place you love isn't here at all, it's either
      because we haven't visited yet (write in) or because it didn't clear the
      bar.
    </p>

    <h3>The photo metadata</h3>
    <p>
      Hero photos are shot on site. Where available, the photo metadata pill
      shows the visit timestamp from the original file. Stock photography is
      never used.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <h2>What's coming</h2>
    <p class="owen-input">
      <span class="owen-input-label">[OWEN INPUT NEEDED: roadmap teaser]</span>
      A short note on where bhamranked is going. Food and drink is the start.
      Services categories (home, personal, retail) come later in 2026 once the
      food vertical has earned its trust. Anything else you want to flag for a
      reader who's wondering whether this is a one-piece project or something
      ongoing.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="cta-card">
      <h3>Questions about a specific piece?</h3>
      <p>
        Corrections, factual errors, and ownership changes go to the editorial
        contact lane. Businesses asking to be considered for a visit have
        their own lane. The contact page keeps them separate.
      </p>
      <a href="/contact" class="cta-link">Contact bhamranked &rarr;</a>
    </div>
  </section>
</TrustPageLayout>
